\begin{definition}[Simon's collision finder]
Let $\mathsf{RP}=\{\mathsf{RP}_{\lambda}\}_{\lambda}$ be a permutation oracle. The \emph{collision finder} $\mathsf{Coll}^{\mathsf{RP}}$ is the oracle that, on input a circuit $C$ whose gates are ordinary Boolean gates, $\mathsf{RP}$-gates and (recursively) $\mathsf{Coll}$-gates, samples a uniformly random point $x$ in the domain of $C$, then samples a uniformly random $x'$ with $C(x')=C(x)$, and returns $(x,x')$. Its answers are fixed in advance by independent uniform choices, so that "a measure one of collision-finders $\mathsf{Coll}^{\mathsf{RP}}$" refers to the product measure over these choices.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$\varepsilon$-secure one-way function]
An oracle $\mathsf F$ \emph{implements an $\varepsilon$-secure one-way function} if no oracle-aided PPT machine $B$ with access to $\mathsf F$ inverts $\mathsf F(x)$ on a uniformly random $x\in\{0,1\}^{\lambda}$ with probability better than $\varepsilon(\lambda)$, for all sufficiently large $\lambda$.
\end{definition}
